===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|de|gmh|kuoche}}, from {{inh|de|goh|kuohho}}, from {{inh|de|gem-pro|*kōkô}}. Compare {{cog|nl|koek}}.

====Noun====
{{de-noun|m|dim=Küchlein,Küchelchen}}

# [[pie]]; [[cake]]; [[tart]] {{gloss|foodstuff made of baked dough, other than bread}}

=====Usage notes=====
* {{m|de||Kuchen}} can refer to almost any foodstuff that is made of dough, baked in a pan or oven, and considered fit for consumption by itself (unlike bread). Which sorts are in fact called {{m|de||Kuchen}} and which are not, is mainly a matter of tradition, for which no clear rule can be given. For example, {{m|de|Pizza}} is not included in the term, unlike the rather similar {{m|de|Zwiebelkuchen|t=onion tart}} and {{m|de|Flammkuchen}}. While most {{m|de||Kuchen}} have pieces cut from them, the northern German {{m|de|Schmalzkuchen}} are bite-sized. Similarly, most {{m|de||Kuchen}} are made of flour, but {{m|de|Reibekuchen}} are made of potatoes.
* All that said, the prototypical {{m|de||Kuchen}} is a fairly large, sweet pastry similar to the {{m+|en|cake}}.

